For mid-to-senior Kubernetes interviews, Autoscaling is one of the most frequently asked topics. You should know not only HPA, but also VPA, Cluster Autoscaler, KEDA, metrics pipeline, and production challenges.

1. Why Autoscaling?

Goal: Automatically adjust resources based on demand.

Without autoscaling:

Low traffic → wasted resources
High traffic → application outage

Autoscaling solves:

Cost optimization
Performance
High availability
2. Types of Autoscaling
A. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)

Scales Pods horizontally.

Example:

Before:

Plain Text
frontend
├── pod1
└── pod2

Traffic increases:

Plain Text
frontend
├── pod1
├── pod2
├── pod3
├── pod4
└── pod5

HPA changes:

YAML
spec:
replicas: 2 --> 5

Interview:

What does HPA scale?

Deployment
StatefulSet
ReplicaSet
Custom resources

It NEVER scales containers.

B. Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)

Changes CPU/Memory requests and limits.

Before:

YAML
requests:
cpu: 100m
memory: 128Mi

After:

YAML
requests:
cpu: 500m
memory: 1Gi

Pod gets recreated.

Interview:

Can VPA resize running pods?

Usually no.

Pod restart required.

C. Cluster Autoscaler (CA)

Adds or removes Kubernetes nodes.

Example:

Plain Text
Node1 -> Full
Node2 -> Full

New pod cannot schedule:

Plain Text
Pending Pod

Cluster Autoscaler:

Plain Text
Adds Node3

Pending pod gets scheduled.

Works with:

AWS ASG
Azure VMSS
GCP Managed Instance Groups
